WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.381
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.396 --> 00:00:09.020
SPEAKER: On the back
office, perhaps we

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:12.890
can even create a net
new capability for HR

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.250
where we collect feedback on
our colleagues and employees

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.770
as they do their work,
and we can collate that.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:20.870
We can just ping that
information to a bot

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:24.440
and collate that information as
we approach the review cycle.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.400
So these are ways that chat bots
are actually being used today.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.560
So hopefully you would agree
with some of these use cases.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.750
So next, I want to go
through a framework that

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.390
might help developers
and executives think

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.010
about chat bots.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.740
So first, I want to
just explain what

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.510
the different axes represent.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:48.720
So on the y-axis,
you have the domain,

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.790
where you start with closed
domain chat bots that

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:55.860
handle one task, are very
specific to one thing,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.730
versus open domain chat bots,
which ostensibly can talk

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.870
about anything under the sun.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:05.940
So it's almost a philosophical
notion, in a way.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.550
And then on the modeling
front along the x-axis,

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:12.000
you see retrieval-based bots
versus generative-based bots.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.290
And so retrieval-based bots
retrieve pre-canned responses

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.480
or they retrieve
information from a database,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.790
while generative bots have
actually learned something

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.579
about the data that
it was trained on,

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:24.120
and they're actually
generating text,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.230
or perhaps you might
say generating speech

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:27.750
as a generative process.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.060
But let's go through these
one quadrant at a time

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.340
and try to understand
a couple of examples.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.880
So in the first
quadrant, let's take

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.390
ordering a pizza as an example.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:39.780
This is pretty straightforward.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.988
There are only so many ways
that you can order pizza.

00:01:41.988 --> 00:01:44.870
There are only so many different
toppings that you can put on.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.130
It has to be
delivered somewhere.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.820
And so the point here is
that it's closed domain.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.577
It's a pretty well-defined
task that needs to occur.

00:01:53.577 --> 00:01:55.160
And it's retrieval-based
because these

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.930
are well-defined
conversations, and we

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:01.780
can retrieve an answer to
everything that the user says.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.500
Moving along the x-axis
towards something slightly more

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.580
complex, you have
a generative model.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.729
And so you may have
heard about Google Duplex

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.420
announced at Google I/O a
couple weeks or months back.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:17.040
And this is using mostly machine
learning and deep learning.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.250
And what it's doing is it's--

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:24.750
Google Duplex is able to
make restaurant reservations

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.050
and hair appointment
bookings for. you,

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.961
and it's also able to do
this in a very dynamic way.

00:02:31.961 --> 00:02:33.960
So if you heard some of
the phone conversations,

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.020
you heard about
how dynamic it was

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:37.940
in being able to
book that for you.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.140
So I'd consider that
a generative process.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.450
But it's closed domain,
because just because it's

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.700
able to make a restaurant
reservation for me does not

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.630
mean that it can help me
assemble my Ikea furniture.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.357
And then in the lower
left-hand corner,

00:02:51.357 --> 00:02:52.940
this is a bit of a
thought experiment,

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:57.040
and so I hope I can convince
you that this is actually

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:57.640
impossible.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.830
And the idea here
is that something

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.160
that's retrieval-based
cannot be open domain.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:06.670
And the idea here is that you
can't anticipate everything

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:10.390
that the user is going
to ask you and anticipate

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.640
every response that you
would have to provide

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.140
for any conversation.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.790
Open domain really
means just that-- we

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.510
can talk about anything.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.060
And so I hope I
don't offend anybody

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:22.240
by using expert systems
here as an example.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.070
Because the idea
behind expert systems

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.290
is to encode information
about the world

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.090
and then be able to
reason about that

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:29.872
and use that in
a productive way.

00:03:29.872 --> 00:03:31.330
But there's always
something that's

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.050
not encoded in the database.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:36.460
Now, to make the
point really clear--

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.830
so perhaps I go to
Fathom and say, hey,

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:41.970
can you get me the
bumblebee cat GIF?

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.710
And the bot says, I don't
think I've seen that one.

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:46.732
And I say, you never
have the latest ones.

00:03:46.732 --> 00:03:48.190
You never have the
latest cat GIFs.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:51.370
And it apologizes because
you can't possibly

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:55.160
keep up with everything
that a user might ask for

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.840
or every conversation
that could take place.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:02.370
So hopefully that
makes the point clear.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.900
And then moving on
from there is something

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.780
that I think a lot of people
in this room, and perhaps

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.980
at all the research
conferences, are interested in,

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.150
which is Artificial General
Intelligence, or AGI.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:17.120
This is the hardest, and this is
where research is taking place.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.600
And this would involve a
generative model, a generative

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.910
based bot that's open domain.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:25.750
Now, we don't really know
how to imbibe machines

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:27.610
with the same qualities
that humans have.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:31.150
We're very deft at
taking new information,

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.180
incorporating that into our
knowledge and understanding

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.740
of the world, and
then using that

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.656
in new and innovative ways.

00:04:37.656 --> 00:04:39.280
And for any non-native
English speakers

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.310
here, maybe you've never
heard the word "deft" before.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:43.810
But you can probably
already start

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.730
to make some predictions
that it means

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.710
in a clever, skillful way.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:50.410
And it's this quality
that we don't know how

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.055
to encode into machines yet--

00:04:52.055 --> 00:04:54.430
where you take something you've
never encountered before,

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.670
you reason about it, and you
extend your understanding

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.530
of the world.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:02.470
And so an example of this,
for any Isaac Asimov fans

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:05.260
out there, is Robby the Robot.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:08.950
And so what we're talking
about is the positronic brain.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:10.910
And I say that with a
little tongue in cheek.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.330
But yeah, this would
be an idea of something

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:17.470
that's artificially
generally intelligent.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.440
So just to be clear, this
is where Fathom Bot sits.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.540
You might argue that
something like Google,

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:26.260
or any kind of search-based
system, is more open domain.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:29.577
But I think that needs
to be reserved for only

00:05:29.577 --> 00:05:30.660
the most advanced systems.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:38.020
So hopefully this is helpful
for, again, both developers

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:40.501
and business thinkers
alike in thinking

00:05:40.501 --> 00:05:42.250
about what capabilities
they need to build

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:45.620
into their next chat bot.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.260
So the bottom line
here is bots are

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.219
really powerful in
well-defined domains.

00:05:49.219 --> 00:05:51.010
We're still figuring
out how to extend them

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:54.495
to many different tasks.

00:05:54.495 --> 00:05:55.870
Now, the second
section is around

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:57.400
how bots are being built today.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:01.150
So as promised, we just talked
about some of the use cases.

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:02.860
And now we'll talk
a bit about how

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:06.370
bots are actually being built.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.630
But first, I think we can
go ahead and do a demo.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.100
So this is the Fathom Bot.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:19.810
And so we can start by
saying, hey, Fathom.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:23.560
And what we'll get
back is a response

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.960
to corral users and
start to educate people

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.900
about how they can use the
bot and what it is useful for.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.880
I think it's important
to educate users.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:32.920
But they're definitely
going to test your bot,

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:34.294
and you're going
to get questions

00:06:34.294 --> 00:06:35.260
like, how old are you.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.470
In bot years, I'm just a kid.

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:44.245
And then you'll get questions
like, can you get smarter.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:57.010
Yes, but it depends on
how much PwC invests.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:07:00.610
This is currently under
active development.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:03.250
And then you'll probably want
to do something more useful,

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:06.260
like search for one of
your colleague's profiles.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:09.190
So let's take Adrian
Boda as an example.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:11.620
And this is actually
his profile,

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:13.690
so please don't spam him.

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:17.020
Adrian is actually
a really nice guy.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:20.620
And so then we may want to
retrieve some information

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.820
about one of our
clients or a company,

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.210
and so we can
search for Alphabet.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.660
And we can pull back
that company card.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:34.860
I'm not sure if the
internet is slow here.

00:07:43.054 --> 00:07:44.466
There we go.

00:07:44.466 --> 00:07:45.840
So if we wanted
to, we could then

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.150
click into that
company's profile.

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:50.850
And I think the end
state of something

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:55.330
like this would be to reason
about that information.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:59.760
So what projects is
Adrian on, or what

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.382
projects do we have going
on with Alphabet right now?

00:08:02.382 --> 00:08:03.840
And so that would
be the end state.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.550
And this uses some
mild machine learning,

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:08.170
and I'll explain that when
we get back to the slides.

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:10.964
But now I want to show you some
more explicit machine learning

00:08:10.964 --> 00:08:11.630
that we've used.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:17.010
So if we engage the FAQ model,
I'll show you how we're-- and I

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.470
should be clear that we've
just engaged a certain intent.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.136
And I'll explain that
when we're talking

00:08:21.136 --> 00:08:22.450
about building these bots.

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.120
But maybe we have
a question that you

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.550
might find in an FAQ sheet.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:30.660
And so what we might
ask about is CEO action

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:34.120
on diversity and inclusion.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:36.470
Because PwC has been really
focused on this this year.

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:38.925
Our CEO has been really
front and center on this.

00:08:38.925 --> 00:08:40.299
And so what you
see is that we're

00:08:40.299 --> 00:08:44.080
matching on a number of
similar questions and answers

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:46.390
in our question and answer base.

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:49.000
And so the idea here
is that we can directly

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.060
address users' questions without
having to have them click

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:53.920
around in the search portal.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:56.650
And so this is using
machine learning.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.000
We've matched on this question.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.490
It gives you the answer,
cites its source.

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:02.806
And then this is really an
artifact of development,

00:09:02.806 --> 00:09:04.180
but it just gives
you this notion

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:10.480
of there is a goodness of
fit or a goodness of match

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.250
that you can discern
how confident you are

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:16.660
in putting forward the answer.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:20.030
So I'll mention that when
I talk about thresholds.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:21.700
But what I'm really
excited to show

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:23.950
you guys today is something
that I think really

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:25.490
moves the user experience.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:29.810
And if I do a general
search for something

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:32.974
related to
international tariffs,

00:09:32.974 --> 00:09:34.640
because that's making
a lot of headlines

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.500
lately, what it's
going to do is it's

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.630
going to go and retrieve
a bunch of articles

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:40.717
from the Fathom database.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:46.908
It's not able to connect
to the Fathom database.

00:09:46.908 --> 00:09:48.200
I'll give it one more try.

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:04.780
Sorry, guys.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:07.217
What's the first
rule of live demos?

00:10:07.217 --> 00:10:07.800
Don't do them.

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.640
I'll give it one
more try, but I don't

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.130
want to belabor the
point, because I'll

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:17.890
talk a bit more about it
later in the presentation.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:20.692
I'm also thinking that
this is public right now,

00:10:20.692 --> 00:10:22.900
and I'm wondering if there
are people in the audience

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:24.940
or remotely that are
actually hitting this bot

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:28.150
and just overloading
it right now.

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:30.190
I thought that
was a possibility.

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:33.725
I was just testing this
before I came up here.

00:10:33.725 --> 00:10:34.850
Let's try it one last time.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:43.460
Well, apologies for that.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:45.260
So what I was going
to try to show

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:48.770
was the ability to
do a general search.

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.080
We retrieve some
documents, and then we

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.560
ask a follow-up question
about those documents.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:57.290
And so this uses a machine
comprehension model.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:59.630
And so I'll talk a
bit more about that

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:01.470
as we move through
the presentation.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.230
So I think with that, we
should probably just move back

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:03.813
to the slides.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:09.490
OK.

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:13.529
So a bit about
what you just saw.

00:11:13.529 --> 00:11:15.070
These are the four
things that you're

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:16.450
going to have to solve
for no matter what

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:17.660
bot that you're building.

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:20.000
And so this is a bit of
a conceptual framework,

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.940
and I think it's helpful for
any developer to understand.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.550
So starting with
intent detection, what

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:29.320
I'll do when I go
through these slides

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:31.090
is I'll talk a bit
about the challenge

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:32.590
that we're talking about.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:34.060
A bit about--

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:37.810
I'll show you an example and
then talk about a few solutions

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.810
using vendor solutions
or open source solutions

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:42.370
if that's more your speed.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:45.190
So for this example,
the intent detection

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:47.254
is what does the user want.

00:11:47.254 --> 00:11:49.420
So if we're looking for the
weather, it's just that.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:51.490
The intent is to
get the weather.

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:54.340
If you look at this example
on the screen, info on Adrian

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:56.830
Boda, it is intent
classification

00:11:56.830 --> 00:12:00.730
where you're trying to determine
what the user is looking for.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:02.050
And it's not a company search.

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:04.959
It's not a people search.

00:12:04.959 --> 00:12:07.000
Or it's not a company
search or a general search.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:08.380
It's a people search.

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:11.350
And so if you think about that,
it's just a text classification

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:11.870
task.

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.740
And you can use your favorite
text classification pipeline,

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:16.900
TFIDF to random forest.

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:18.610
Or if you have more
data, you might

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:21.627
go with something more
sophisticated, like an LSTM.

00:12:21.627 --> 00:12:23.710
Or if you don't want to
build something like that,

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:27.280
you can use something--
rules-ish-based approach,

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:31.270
like Dialogflow, or the
NLP API, which hopefully I

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:34.700
can explain on the next slide.

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:37.240
So the second challenge is
going to be entity extraction.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.850
What information do we need
to extract from the text

00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:42.500
in order to be able to
fulfill the request?

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:46.540
And so what we see here
is-- and then one sub-bullet

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:48.340
on that is can we
rely on dialogue

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:51.531
flow for proper nouns, so
people, places, and things

00:12:51.531 --> 00:12:52.030
like that.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:55.450
Because you have to
train the Dialogflow

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.880
agent to be able to
detect these proper nouns.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:00.850
And it's very difficult to
enumerate all of the employees

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:03.160
at your company in Dialogflow.

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:07.510
And so here are two ways that
this may go off the rails.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:11.110
In the first example, somebody
might say info on Adrian Boda.

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.900
And maybe you get the intent,
which is to query for a person,

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:15.880
but you don't
extract the entity,

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:17.860
which is underlined in red.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:20.580
And so one solution here
is to use follow-ups.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:22.240
So you might say,
hey, I think you're

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:26.510
looking for people search,
but I didn't get the name.

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:28.240
So what name are
you looking for?

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:30.730
And then you can
proceed that way

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:32.380
when the user provides the name.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:34.900
Another situation is where you
don't have a lot of contacts

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:37.510
at all, and you're working
with proper nouns, something

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:38.640
like Alphabet.

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:41.560
You might not get the
intent or the entity.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:44.830
And so one solution here is to
use something like the NLP API.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:47.110
So you head out to
the NLP API, determine

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:48.940
what entity was extracted.

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:50.650
And if it's an
organization, you now

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:53.530
have the company that
you're searching for

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:56.890
as well as the intent,
which is a company search.

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:58.660
And so what we're
talking about here

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:01.180
is named entity recognition
models, or NER models.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:04.060
And Dialogflow
does this natively.

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:06.440
The NLP API does this natively.

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:08.750
But if you're more into
the open source scene,

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.890
the Stanford NER
Tagger or Spacey

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:13.900
or any number of
open source tools

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:18.070
will allow you to
accomplish this task.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:20.530
The next challenge
is state management.

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:23.740
The idea here is where are
we in the conversation.

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:27.460
And anytime you have
a conversation that

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:29.215
takes on more than one turn--

00:14:29.215 --> 00:14:31.840
in other words, the user asks a
question like-- and by the way,

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:33.700
this was what I was
going to try to show,

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:37.040
was where you have a search
like international tariffs.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.160
It finds some
documents and asks,

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:40.810
do you have any
additional questions.

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:42.160
I say yes.

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:44.424
And then we ask about--

00:14:44.424 --> 00:14:46.090
I don't know-- what
are the implications

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:50.770
of international tariffs
on manufacturing.

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:54.490
And then it will give
you a direct answer.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:57.610
Now, I also want to mention that
machine learning hasn't really

00:14:57.610 --> 00:14:58.900
been able to solve this yet.

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:02.050
And so I'll link you to a paper
here called "End-to-End Task

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:04.120
Completion Neural
Dialogue Systems"

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:08.050
where they attempt to use
a fully differentiable deep

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:10.990
learning model to be able
to solve the task of booking

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:12.100
a movie ticket.

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:14.380
And it's with mixed results.

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:16.720
So I guess that brings
me to the solutions.

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:19.090
Dialogflow is terrific
for this, architecting

00:15:19.090 --> 00:15:21.174
these conversational flows.

00:15:21.174 --> 00:15:22.840
And then another thing
that I'll mention

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:25.690
is Mongo or some sort
of external store.

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:28.840
You can imagine interacting
with your user across time

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:30.970
and across multiple platforms.

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:35.890
And so you need some notion of
cache or persisting that user's

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:38.129
profile across interactions.

00:15:38.129 --> 00:15:39.670
And then the final
thing is hopefully

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:41.336
machine learning will
make some advances

00:15:41.336 --> 00:15:43.270
in this in the near
question mark future.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:50.050
The final challenge to be solved
for any bot, assuming it's

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:52.840
retrieval-based-- so the
distinction I made earlier

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:57.919
would be retrieval-based
versus generative-based models.

00:15:57.919 --> 00:15:59.710
When you think about
information retrieval,

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:01.970
there are two sources
of information.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:03.940
One is structured sources.

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:05.320
The other is unstructured.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:09.940
And so structured sources
are things like CSV files.

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:13.840
You can think of them like
SQL queries or API calls.

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:17.470
But unstructured sources require
a bit more deep learning,

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:19.930
machine learning, data science.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.640
And that's the stuff
that I get excited about.

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:26.620
And so what you see here is
an unstructured text document.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:29.350
And that was really the
focus of our project, which

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:32.680
was to retrieve information
from unstructured text sources,

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:35.410
so similar to the way that
I was showing the FAQ model,

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:40.130
where we're able to
answer questions as such.

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:42.880
And so some solutions here
are to use machine learning

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:46.120
models and deep learning models,
or even something more naive,

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:49.790
like a TFIDF model,
if you have less data.

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:53.380
And then some of the easier
ones are SQL queries and API

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:54.100
queries.

00:16:54.100 --> 00:16:56.650
And I'll also call out QANet,
because that was the model

00:16:56.650 --> 00:17:00.760
that we had running in our
Fathom Bot, which is a machine

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:01.930
comprehension model.

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:04.760
Google published that
paper a few months back.

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:07.359
And I think it's still number
one on the squad leader board.

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:09.829
And I'll explain a bit
more about all that

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:11.210
in just a moment.

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:15.020
So this is probably why a lot
of you guys attended this talk.

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:17.470
And so I want to take
a moment to just talk

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:19.510
through the architecture
and what this actually

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:22.480
looks like on Google Cloud.

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:25.780
Starting with the user in
the upper right corner,

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:30.010
the user is trying to
interact with Dialogflow.

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:32.530
They make a hop to
App Engine, which

00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:35.680
makes the call to Dialogflow.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:38.800
And Dialogflow evaluates
the user's request,

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:41.530
determines how to route it,
determines what information

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:43.820
needs to be extracted
out of that,

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:46.180
and then sends that to what
I call the broker, which is

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:47.420
this guy right in the middle.

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:50.080
And this is a bit
of a Russian doll.

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:54.070
We have a Python Flask
app running in Docker,

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:55.990
running on Kubernetes.

00:17:55.990 --> 00:18:00.877
And so I'm sure this is a pretty
common design pattern for some

00:18:00.877 --> 00:18:01.960
of the folks in this room.

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:04.550
But what I really like is
the elasticity of this.

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:06.670
And so the idea
here is that you get

00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:08.500
all the benefits of
the horizontal scaling

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:11.590
as more users come online.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:12.490
And so the idea--

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:14.170
I guess I should
be clear and say

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:17.320
that the broker's
responsibility is

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:19.810
to ping all these different
APIs, to go out and do

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:23.620
fulfillment, as it's called, in
the Dialogflow documentation.

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:27.910
And so one source of
fulfillment is the PwC database,

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:29.950
where we're retrieving
some of that information

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:33.380
about our colleagues and
companies and clients.

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:37.504
And then we also have
some GPU-enabled instances

00:18:37.504 --> 00:18:38.420
running on Kubernetes.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:39.880
So we've got some
TensorFlow models

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:43.450
running on GPU-enabled instances
for some of these tasks,

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:47.080
like information retrieval,
question answer models.

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:51.640
And then finally, you can use
external storage like Mongo,

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:53.650
or you might even
hack it with something

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:56.650
like Cloud Storage, where you're
just retrieving information

00:18:56.650 --> 00:18:59.050
based on conversation IDs.

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:04.390
So this is, in short,
what is architected

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:06.220
on the Cloud platform.

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:10.630
And a couple of tips that might
be useful to somebody that's

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:13.130
going down this road--

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:15.490
once you understand
what Dialogflow does,

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:19.750
I would encourage you to look at
the Python Dialogflow package.

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:21.910
Once you really understand
what Dialogflow does,

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:25.900
you can and should script just
about everything with respect

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:28.660
to bot creation and updates.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.012
Gcloud is obviously
a great tool.

00:19:31.012 --> 00:19:31.720
It's your friend.

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:36.040
You can use it to build
these Kubernetes clusters.

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:37.480
You can basically
use it to build

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:38.710
your entire infrastructure.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:41.200
And so I would
definitely encourage

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:46.030
that to be your source of
documentation versus creating

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:48.490
documentation sheets
where somebody

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:50.980
has to go into the user
interface and click around.

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:54.490
It's just too prone to
error, as we've found.

00:19:54.490 --> 00:19:56.410
Establish a robust
testing framework.

00:19:56.410 --> 00:20:00.350
So this becomes a
quite complex system.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:06.140
What happens is you've got a
noisy input coming from users.

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:07.800
Dialogflow is evaluating that.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:09.550
You're trying to
understand how Dialogflow

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:12.160
is going to behave under
various circumstances,

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:13.660
and then you're
trying to understand

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:16.270
how fulfillment
is going to behave

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:17.840
under certain circumstances.

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:20.620
So you need to really have
a robust testing framework

00:20:20.620 --> 00:20:23.530
to understand where things
might be going off the rails,

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:25.270
if and when they do.

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:28.332
And then finally, on team
Docker and Kubernetes,

00:20:28.332 --> 00:20:30.290
I probably don't have to
say much more on that.

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:34.030
But I just like it as
a development pattern.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:36.430
So the key things here
are machine learning

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:38.920
is already pretty present
in most chat bots today,

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:42.220
and GCP has all the
necessary tools to do this.

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:45.700
So now let's move into some
of the intelligence layer,

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:48.430
as I was discussing before,
with machine learning.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:51.700
And again, as promised, we've
talked about some use cases.

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:54.451
We've talked a bit about
how bots are actually built.

00:20:54.451 --> 00:20:56.950
And now we're going to talk a
bit about how machine learning

00:20:56.950 --> 00:21:00.230
is layered into this bot.

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:02.870
So these were three
information retrieval tasks

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:05.440
that we thought about
during this project.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:09.790
And so what are each
of these in a word?

00:21:09.790 --> 00:21:12.760
So document retrieval--
you've got a query,

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:14.710
and you're looking for
relevant documents.

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:16.210
It's basically what Google does.

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:18.620
We have a query like
international tariffs.

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:21.370
We should be able to
retrieve documents at PwC

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:25.540
that are related to this query.

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:27.190
The second task,
question answering,

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:31.180
is taking questions that you
might see on an FAQ document,

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:35.470
and being able to take a query,
and find a similar question

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:37.980
or find the answer
to that user's query.

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:39.730
So an example would
be, what should I know

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:41.530
about international tariffs.

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:43.690
And the response would
be the matched question,

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:46.690
so what tariffs
have been proposed,

00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:48.580
which it was matched to.

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:50.920
And then ultimately, the
user is looking for something

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:53.670
like this, like the answer.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:55.440
And then machine
comprehension, which

00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:57.780
I think is the most
sophisticated of all these,

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:01.830
is where you're answering
a user's question

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:04.740
by retrieving a very
specific span of text

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:07.200
from some unstructured
text document.

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:08.910
So an example was--

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:11.160
and this is what I wanted
to show during the demo,

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:12.784
but the query would
have been something

00:22:12.784 --> 00:22:15.482
like, what should manufacturers
prepare for after tariffs.

00:22:15.482 --> 00:22:17.190
And the response would
be something like,

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.960
higher material costs.

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:21.970
So again, these were
three of the tasks.

00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:25.420
We focused on the latter two,
so question and answering

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:26.682
and machine comprehension.

00:22:26.682 --> 00:22:28.390
So we can double click
into each of those

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:30.806
to get a better sense of how
we're actually building that.

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:36.010
So question answering-- there's
two ways to think about it.

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:38.670
One is question to question,
where you're using something

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:41.370
like a TFIDF-based
model, and you can just

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:44.610
measure the cosine similarity
between two vectors,

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:46.650
and you'll get something
like a match score.

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:49.590
And the idea here is you
take a question like this,

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:51.296
and you match it to
all of the questions

00:22:51.296 --> 00:22:52.920
that you have in your
database, and you

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:54.660
look for the best match.

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:57.090
And then by default,
you just have the answer

00:22:57.090 --> 00:22:58.380
as your lookup value.

00:22:58.380 --> 00:23:01.362
So that's hopefully
pretty straightforward.

00:23:01.362 --> 00:23:03.070
Now, the other way of
thinking about it--

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:04.930
and this is often when
you have more data.

00:23:04.930 --> 00:23:08.410
And we've trained models
with hundreds of thousands

00:23:08.410 --> 00:23:10.990
of examples, and we've seen
deep learning models start

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:13.480
to really excel here where
you go from a question

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:14.650
to an answer.

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:16.570
So the user says something
like, what should I

00:23:16.570 --> 00:23:19.300
know about
international tariffs,

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:22.870
and you're trying to
match to an answer.

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:24.400
And so the deep
learning model is

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:26.140
going through the
answer column now

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:30.610
and trying to match that query
to the appropriate answer.

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:35.020
And I think this is probably
obvious for a number of folks,

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:37.000
but you might want
to cite your source

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:40.060
and any other information that
might be helpful to the user

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:42.910
to be able to do their
own further investigation.

00:23:42.910 --> 00:23:44.530
Now, the final
piece of this talk

00:23:44.530 --> 00:23:45.880
is around machine comprehension.

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:47.920
And I really like
the way that one

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:51.070
of the co-authors of the
SQuAD paper described it.

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:53.920
And he says it's "the ability
to read a piece of text

00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:55.810
and then answer
questions about it."

00:23:55.810 --> 00:23:58.060
And this is really
challenging for machines.

00:23:58.060 --> 00:24:02.710
And to be clear, the SQuAD paper
refers to the Stanford Question

00:24:02.710 --> 00:24:05.020
Answering Dataset,
which has become

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:08.230
the de facto benchmark for
machine comprehension models.

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:10.720
And so QANet, as I
mentioned earlier, I think,

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:15.070
is still ranked number
one for that dataset.

00:24:15.070 --> 00:24:17.170
And so again, to be
abundantly clear,

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:20.860
we're talking about
selecting an answer span

00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:23.530
from a provided text passage.

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:26.170
And so if we look at the
picture on the right, which

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:29.710
is taken from the SQuAD paper,
you see there's a passage.

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:31.990
These are Wikipedia articles.

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:34.690
And then the users have
posed a bunch of questions

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:36.730
against that article.

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:38.920
And they've also
highlighted the answer.

00:24:38.920 --> 00:24:42.220
And so the first
question is, what

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:45.170
causes precipitation to
fall, answer being gravity.

00:24:45.170 --> 00:24:47.330
And you can see that
it's listed up above.

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:49.960
And so that should also
tell you how these datasets

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:53.410
are structured where you
have a passage, a query,

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:54.466
and an answer.

00:24:54.466 --> 00:24:56.590
And with that, we can build
machine learning models

00:24:56.590 --> 00:25:00.250
to take in passages
and questions,

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:02.800
and hopefully put
forward what it thinks

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:06.330
is the most likely answer.

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:08.610
And so for anyone
that thinks they've

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:11.580
solved the task of
machine comprehension,

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:15.100
I would encourage you to take
a look at this question here,

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:19.860
which says, "which technology
was developed most recently?"

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:21.330
And to us, the
answer is obviously

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:22.950
A, the cellular telephone.

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:26.640
But for the guy on the left,
this is a difficult task.

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:29.640
We don't really know how
to imbibe machine learning

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:33.270
models with this notion of
common sense or real world

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:34.320
reasoning.

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:35.691
And the information is there.

00:25:35.691 --> 00:25:37.440
We just don't really
know how to encode it

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:39.210
into these machine
learning models,

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:42.180
whereas it's very trivial for
the guys on the right, which

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:45.660
is a picture from the
movie "Dumb and Dumber."

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:48.960
And I'll leave you with
one little nugget here.

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:50.880
If you're interested
in these topics,

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:54.270
I really enjoy listening to a
podcast called "Data Skeptic,"

00:25:54.270 --> 00:25:56.340
hosted by a guy
named Kyle Polich.

00:25:56.340 --> 00:25:58.774
And he did an episode
called "The Imitation Game"

00:25:58.774 --> 00:26:00.690
where he interviewed
some folks from the Allen

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:05.310
Institute and talks about
this dataset and this task.

00:26:05.310 --> 00:26:07.680
Fresh off the press--

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:10.500
last month's SQuAD,
the SQuAD team,

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:11.880
released a second dataset.

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:14.610
And I think they're addressing
what I think is a pretty

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:19.590
important problem, which is
to abstain when presented with

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:21.324
a question that
cannot be answered.

00:26:21.324 --> 00:26:23.490
In other words, if the model
is not confident enough

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:26.610
or couldn't find the answer
in the provided text passages,

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:28.680
we need to know that
we're not confident enough

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:29.940
to put forward the answer.

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:32.400
And right now, I'm having to
use things like thresholds

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:34.202
to be able to
determine if I've got

00:26:34.202 --> 00:26:35.910
enough information to
answer the question

00:26:35.910 --> 00:26:40.770
or not, or if I'm confident
enough to answer that question.

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:42.320
So for any
practitioners out there,

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:45.770
you know that transfer learning
can only take you so far.

00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:49.880
SQuAD was from the
Wikipedia domain.

00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:51.590
I'm working in the PwC domain.

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:54.230
So transferring a model
train on Wikipedia

00:26:54.230 --> 00:26:57.380
will only take me
so far for PwC.

00:26:57.380 --> 00:26:59.460
Labeling data is expensive.

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:02.450
So as much as I'd love $20,000
to go out to Mechanical Turk

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:04.190
and label a bunch
of data, I'm not

00:27:04.190 --> 00:27:05.870
going to get approved for that.

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:08.090
And so we need to
think about how

00:27:08.090 --> 00:27:11.600
we generate data in either
a semi-supervised or a fully

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:12.930
unsupervised way.

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:15.920
And so what I want to
share here is something

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:18.710
that we would be exploring
if we had more time.

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:20.900
And this paper is called
[? "Sin ?] [? Net." ?] And

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:23.390
if you look at the 1, 2,
and 3 on the screen here,

00:27:23.390 --> 00:27:27.380
the idea here is that we
can take the SQuAD data set.

00:27:27.380 --> 00:27:30.470
We've got the passages, the
questions, and the answers.

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:33.380
And we can train the model to
generate interesting questions

00:27:33.380 --> 00:27:35.180
and answers given the passage.

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:37.580
And we know what those
questions and answers are

00:27:37.580 --> 00:27:39.510
because we have labeled data.

00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:41.660
So once we train a
model to do that,

00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:45.650
we can feed in PwC documents,
and take those passages,

00:27:45.650 --> 00:27:49.400
and generate questions and
answers in the PwC domain.

00:27:49.400 --> 00:27:51.050
And then you close
the training loop

00:27:51.050 --> 00:27:53.510
by going back to the
machine comprehension model

00:27:53.510 --> 00:27:56.210
and fine-tuning that model
on a combination of the SQuAD

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.970
data and the PwC data
to be able to bring

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:02.330
that model into the PwC domain
and perform a little bit

00:28:02.330 --> 00:28:03.060
better.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:04.040
So these are the
things that you have

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:05.720
to think about on
a day to day to be

00:28:05.720 --> 00:28:07.803
able to actually get these
machine learning models

00:28:07.803 --> 00:28:09.620
up and running.

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:12.160
So I think hopefully, that
was convincing that machine

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:14.200
learning models can
and should improve

00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:15.970
information
retrieval, especially

00:28:15.970 --> 00:28:18.800
for unstructured documents.

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:21.910
So some final
considerations here--

00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:23.440
what data can you collect?

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:25.750
So for the FAQ documents,
we had to round up

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:30.040
all these FAQ sheets and then
parse out those documents,

00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:32.177
extract out the
questions, the answers.

00:28:32.177 --> 00:28:33.760
And I think we wound
up with something

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:35.650
like 20,000
question-answer pairs

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:38.140
that we can then train our
machine learning models with.

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:39.940
I just talked a
couple of slides back

00:28:39.940 --> 00:28:41.920
about the need to
generate or label

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:44.730
data in some circumstances.

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:47.590
These are some things that
you will have to think about.

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:50.680
And then how can we improve
the quality of our results

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:52.190
from user feedback?

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:55.690
So if we're open to capturing
user feedback, was this useful

00:28:55.690 --> 00:28:59.470
or not, how do we then close
the model training loop

00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:04.330
and incorporate that into
our model, improve results?

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:06.070
On the user front,
how confident are

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:07.910
that we have the right answer?

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:11.140
And this comes back to the
notion of thresholding,

00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:13.750
where you have a
probability score associated

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:15.580
with a lot of these models.

00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:17.200
And that should be
tuned to determine

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:20.320
the number of false positives
and also the amount of volume

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:24.930
that you can handle
for incoming queries.

00:29:24.930 --> 00:29:27.160
Be clear about what the
bot can and can't do.

00:29:27.160 --> 00:29:29.300
So when I first started
interacting with the bot,

00:29:29.300 --> 00:29:32.460
it said I'm helpful for
people queries, company

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:34.380
queries, general searches.

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:36.630
And so you have to
coach users, because I

00:29:36.630 --> 00:29:38.790
think the expectations
are set very high,

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:42.150
And I think you're definitely
set up to disappoint users

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:43.680
if you're not clear enough.

00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:45.100
Consider edge cases.

00:29:45.100 --> 00:29:48.664
So your users will try to
push the bots to its limits,

00:29:48.664 --> 00:29:50.580
and you just want to
understand how it's going

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:53.640
to behave in those edge cases.

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:56.610
And this is for a number of
data scientists out in the room.

00:29:56.610 --> 00:29:59.610
I think we all do a good
job defining measures

00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:02.490
of intrinsic evaluation--
so accuracy score,

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:06.554
recall at k, F1 scores, exact
match, all that fun stuff.

00:30:06.554 --> 00:30:08.220
But we also need to
think about measures

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:09.630
of extrinsic evaluation.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:12.750
So how have we moved the needle
for the business in terms

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:15.300
of user time saved,
number of clicks

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:18.330
reduced to find a
piece of information,

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:20.530
or potentially even revenue?

00:30:20.530 --> 00:30:24.720
So if I've done my job
well, this is where you are.

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:27.321
We've talked about
some use cases, how

00:30:27.321 --> 00:30:29.820
bots are being built today, and
a bit about the intelligence

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:30.515
layer.

00:30:30.515 --> 00:30:31.890
That concludes
this talk, and I'm

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:33.431
happy to take
questions at this time.

00:30:33.431 --> 00:30:34.410
Thank you.

00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:37.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

